A||||12||pHoOl||Shift kits, do or don't?||||1||
Z||000000||pHoOl||06-21-2001||10:19 PM||||I was just informed last night that these are only 35 bucks for the fiero.  I have heard of shift kits before, but i'm not exactly sure what they are.  Can anyone give me an explanation (doesn't have to be laymen) and also an idea of how hard this is to install myself.  Or how much it would cost (estimate obviously) for a shop to do it?<P>Thanks all<BR><P>------------------<BR>Ricers are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Songman||06-21-2001||10:53 PM||||Shift kits basically change the way your car shifts. Generally this is done for people who want a harder shift and better performance. In the hot rod V8 world we always did it to 'grab a 2nd' as we said.<P>There are a lot of pros and cons to shift kits, depending on what you want to do. The pros are that the car shifts more for performance than economy and it gives you a real neck jerk when it does shift.<P>The cons are that it shifts more for performance than economy and it gives you a real neck jerk with it does shift.. hehe<P>As you see, it depends on what you want to do with your car... You have to consider that the rough shift can be damaging to parts of your car over time.<P>Kits are fairly easy to install (on regular RWD trannies such as TH400), again depending on which kit and how severe the performance upgrade. Usually it is just a matter of putting a different shield over the valve body and maybe moving a ball into another tube. Personally, I wouldn't try it on a Fiero tranny unless I already had it out and was a little familiar with it. And shops that will do it in your area may be a little tough to find too... But I would say if you have moderate mechanical experience then you should be able to do it if you want.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||pHoOl||06-21-2001||11:35 PM||||Someone told me that a shift kit will make the tranny last longer, and give you better gas mileage.  i guess it's true what they say about things that sound too good to be true.  <P>Anyone have one of these installed?  feedback?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Ricers are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||06-21-2001||11:49 PM||||There are "performance" shift kits and "heavy duty" shift kits made for the Fiero TH-125's. I have the Heavy Duty one. It is available thru Trans-Go. The Performance ones will put more strain on the trans and associated components. The heavy duty kits give you less delay between shifts, stop the big clunk between 3rd and 2nd, and prevent torque converter drain back in hard cornering. The HD kits will improve the longevity of the trans and there is a performance gain due to better shifting curve.<P>Trans Go does not make a performance one for the TH-125. Here is a link to them that will explain what the kit does and how to install it. (I had a shop install mine)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/sk.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.txchange.com/sk.htm</A> <P>The kit # is SK 125 and the decription is below:<BR>GM HD Kit<BR>THM 125 <BR>1980 up <BR>Corrects/prevents/Reduces: <BR>3-2 clunk; lockup shudder; reverse slip; converter drain back delay; <BR>late shifts; soft 1-2; band failure. <P>I would recommend the HD kit. Performance ones can get to be a pain to setup correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||06-22-2001||11:06 AM||||quicker shift on an automatic always mean less wear, theres lots of slippage in the clutch packs. Also feels 'sportier'. I dont know of any downside to shift kit other than loosing the mushy cadillac gear changes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||06-22-2001||03:09 PM||||I am in the process of intalling one on a customers car this very moment - call here at the shop for any questions<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
